## Syntax errors

happens when the code you've written doesn't follow python's grammar rules — a missing colon, mismatched brackets, a typo in a keyword
python catches these before your code even starts running, since it can't parse the file properly in the first place
example: if True
print("hello") result SyntaxError: expected ':'
these are usually the easiest to fix since python points almost exactly at the spot it got confused

## Runtime errors

happens while the program is actually running, after it's already passed the syntax check
the code is grammatically valid python, but something goes wrong when it actually executes
example: x = 10 / 0
result ZeroDivisionError: division by zero
other common ones: TypeError, ValueError, IndexError, KeyError, AttributeError — each one describing a different kind of thing going wrong at the moment it happens

## Logical errors

the trickiest kind — the code runs fine, no error message at all, but it produces the wrong result because the logic itself is off
example: def average(a, b):
return a + b / 2 wrong, division happens before addition average(4, 6)
result 7.0 instead of the expected 5.0
fixing these means checking the logic step by step, usually with print statements or a debugger, since python has no way of knowing your intention was different from what you actually wrote

## Tracebacks

the block of text python prints out when an error happens, showing exactly where and why
reads from bottom to top for the actual error, and top to bottom for the sequence of calls that led there
example of what one looks like: Traceback (most recent call last):
File "main.py", line 3, in <module>
result = divide(10, 0)
File "main.py", line 1, in divide
return a / b
ZeroDivisionError: division by zero
the very last line is the actual error type and message, the lines above show the chain of function calls that got you there — always worth reading that last line first

## try

a block that lets you attempt code that might fail, without crashing the whole program if it does
example: try:
x = 10 / 0
on its own a try block needs at least one except attached to it, otherwise python complains

## except

catches an error if one happens inside the matching try block, and runs its own code instead of letting the program crash
example: try:
x = 10 / 0
except ZeroDivisionError:
print("can't divide by zero")
can catch specific error types, or catch anything at all with a bare except — though catching everything blindly is generally considered bad practice since it can hide bugs you actually wanted to know about
can also grab the error object itself to inspect it: except ZeroDivisionError as e:
print(e)

## else

inside a try block, an else clause runs only if the try block succeeded with no error at all
example: try:
x = 10 / 2
except ZeroDivisionError:
print("failed")
else:
print("succeeded, x is", x) result succeeded, x is 5.0
keeps the "what happens if it worked" code separate from the try block itself, so the except doesn't accidentally catch an error from that follow-up code too

## finally

a block that always runs no matter what, whether the try succeeded, failed, or even if the except itself re-raises something
example: try:
x = 10 / 0
except ZeroDivisionError:
print("failed")
finally:
print("this always runs")
commonly used for cleanup work, like closing a file or a network connection, that needs to happen regardless of whether things went well

## raise

manually triggers an error yourself, instead of waiting for python to hit one naturally
example: def set_age(age):
if age < 0:
raise ValueError("age can't be negative")
return age set_age(-5)
result ValueError: age can't be negative
useful for catching bad input early and giving a clear, specific message, instead of letting the program fail confusingly somewhere further down the line

## Custom exceptions

your own error type, made by creating a class that inherits from Exception
example: class NegativeAgeError(Exception):
pass def set_age(age):
if age < 0:
raise NegativeAgeError("age can't be negative")
worth doing when the built-in error types (ValueError, TypeError, and so on) don't really describe what actually went wrong in your specific program, and a more descriptive name would make debugging faster

## Exception hierarchy

every exception type in python inherits from a common ancestor, BaseException, with Exception sitting just below it as the parent of almost everything you'll actually use
specific errors branch off from there — ZeroDivisionError and OverflowError are both types of ArithmeticError, for instance
example of what that means practically: try:
x = 10 / 0
except ArithmeticError:
print("some kind of maths error")
catching a broader category up the chain will also catch anything more specific underneath it, so except Exception catches almost anything, while except ZeroDivisionError only catches that one exact thing

## Assertions

a quick sanity check using the assert keyword — if the condition is false, it raises an AssertionError immediately
example: def divide(a, b):
assert b != 0, "b cannot be zero"
return a / b divide(10, 0)
result AssertionError: b cannot be zero
meant for catching things that should never happen if the code's logic is correct — not for handling expected, normal errors like bad user input, which is what try/except is for instead

## Defensive programming

writing code that assumes things can and will go wrong, and checks for that ahead of time instead of just hoping for the best
example: def get_first_item(items):
if not items:
return None
return items[0]
includes things like validating input before using it, checking a list isn't empty before indexing into it, and not assuming a file, a network request, or user input will always behave the way you expect

## Debuggers

a tool that lets you pause a running program and look inside it, instead of guessing what's happening from print statements alone
lets you step through code one line at a time, watch how variables change, and inspect the exact state of the program at the moment something goes wrong
VS Code has one built in — set a breakpoint, hit run in debug mode, and it'll pause execution right there

## Breakpoints

a marker you place on a specific line telling the debugger "pause the program here"
in VS Code, click just to the left of the line number and a red dot appears
once the program hits that line while running in debug mode, everything freezes there and you can inspect every variable currently in scope, then step forward one line at a time from that point

## pdb

python's built-in debugger, usable straight from the terminal without needing an editor with debugging support
drop this line wherever you want execution to pause: import pdb; pdb.set_trace()
once it hits that line, you get a small interactive prompt right there in the terminal where you can type variable names to inspect them, or commands like n to move to the next line, c to continue running, and q to quit out entirely

## Logging

a more permanent, structured alternative to scattering print statements everywhere, especially useful once a program gets bigger
example: import logging
logging.basicConfig(level=logging.INFO)
logging.info("starting the program")
logging.warning("something looks off")
logging.error("something failed")
has different severity levels (debug, info, warning, error, critical) so you can filter what actually gets shown, and unlike print statements, log lines can be switched off without deleting them from the code

## Reading error messages properly

the error type itself (ValueError, KeyError, and so on) tells you the category of what went wrong before you even read the rest
the message right after the error type usually spells out specifically what happened, worth reading in full rather than skimming past it
the traceback's last line is the actual error, the lines above it show the path of function calls that led there — read bottom to top for the cause, top to bottom for the journey
the line number given is almost always exactly where to look first, or occasionally the line just above it
treating an error message as information rather than a wall of scary red text is most of what separates someone who gets stuck from someone who fixes it quickly